---
import Icon from '../Icon.astro';
---

<section class="rounded-[1.75rem] border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface-strong)] p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-sm space-y-8">
  <!-- Header -->
  <div class="space-y-3">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] px-3.5 py-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[color:var(--accent)]">
      <Icon name="document" class="w-3.5 h-3.5" />
      <span>Academic Standards & Educational Guidance</span>
    </div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[color:var(--fg)]">
      Understanding Academic Grading Scales & Standards
    </h2>
    <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--muted)]">
      Grading systems vary by country, university, institution, and sometimes program. When possible, use the grading scale shown on your official transcript or your institution's academic regulations.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Important Callout: Which Standard Should I Use? -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--accent)]/30 bg-gradient-to-br from-[color:var(--accent-soft)] via-[color:var(--surface)] to-transparent p-5 sm:p-6 space-y-4">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-base font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)]">
      <Icon name="sparkles" class="w-5 h-5 text-[color:var(--accent)]" />
      <h3>Which Standard Should I Use?</h3>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--muted)]">
      To ensure your calculations are accurate and recognized by your department, follow these verification steps:
    </p>
    <ol class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <li class="rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-3.5 space-y-1">
        <span class="font-bold text-[color:var(--accent)]">1. Check your official transcript</span>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)]">Look at the legend or grading scale printed on the reverse side or header of your official transcript.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-3.5 space-y-1">
        <span class="font-bold text-[color:var(--accent)]">2. Check your university policy</span>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)]">Review your department handbook, syllabus, or registrar's published academic regulations.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-3.5 space-y-1">
        <span class="font-bold text-[color:var(--accent)]">3. Identify the maximum scale</span>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)]">Confirm whether your program operates on a 4.0, 5.0, 7.0, 10.0, percentage, or other maximum scale.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-3.5 space-y-1">
        <span class="font-bold text-[color:var(--accent)]">4. Check the grade-to-point table</span>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)]">Confirm exact point values assigned to letter grades (e.g. whether an A- is 3.70 or if straight letter grades apply).</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-3.5 space-y-1">
        <span class="font-bold text-[color:var(--accent)]">5. Select the matching standard</span>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)]">Choose the standard that corresponds precisely to your institution's published conversion scheme.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-3.5 space-y-1">
        <span class="font-bold text-[color:var(--accent)]">6. Use Custom Standard if unlisted</span>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)]">If no standard matches, select Custom Standard to input your school's exact boundaries and point values.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-3 text-xs font-semibold text-[color:var(--fg)]">
      <span class="text-[color:var(--accent)] font-bold">Important:</span> Do not choose a grading standard solely because it is commonly used in your country.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Warning: Percentage to GPA Conversion is NOT Universal -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-amber-500/30 bg-amber-500/5 p-5 sm:p-6 space-y-2">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-bold text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400">
      <Icon name="alert-circle" class="w-4.5 h-4.5" />
      <h4>Percentage to GPA Conversion is Not Universal</h4>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--muted)]">
      An 80% mark does not automatically equal the same letter grade or GPA at every institution. Percentage-to-grade and percentage-to-GPA conversions depend on the grading policy being used. For example, an 80% score represents First-Class Honours in the UK undergraduate system, a B- or B in many North American 4.0 models, and an A or A+ in other international jurisdictions. Universal or automated mathematical conversions without institutional backing can produce misleading academic results.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Commonly Encountered Systems -->
  <div class="space-y-4">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)]">Commonly Encountered Grading Systems</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)]">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <thead class="border-b border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface-strong)] text-[color:var(--fg)] font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Grading Scale</th>
            <th class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Key Characteristics</th>
            <th class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Where It Is Often Encountered</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-[color:var(--border)] text-[color:var(--muted)]">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4 font-semibold text-[color:var(--fg)]">4.0 GPA Scale</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Grades convert into quality points from 0.00 to 4.00. Many institutions incorporate plus/minus steps (e.g., A = 4.0, A- = 3.7, B+ = 3.3), while others utilize straight letter grades (A = 4.0, B = 3.0).</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Very common across the United States, Canada, Bangladesh, Saudi Arabia, and American-curriculum international programs.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4 font-semibold text-[color:var(--fg)]">5.0 GPA Scale</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Maximum grade point is 5.00. Typically maps letter grades A through F into whole or decimal intervals (A = 5.0, B = 4.0, C = 3.0, D = 2.0, E = 1.0, F = 0.0).</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Widely used across universities in Nigeria (governed by the National Universities Commission) and in specific weighted high school programs.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4 font-semibold text-[color:var(--fg)]">7.0 GPA Scale</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Scale ranges from 0.00 to 7.00, utilizing categories such as High Distinction (7.0), Distinction (6.0), Credit (5.0), Pass (4.0), and Fail (0.0).</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Used by some higher education institutions in Australia, though others utilize Weighted Average Mark (WAM).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4 font-semibold text-[color:var(--fg)]">10.0 GPA / CGPA Scale</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Operates on a 10-point scale with letter bands (O = 10, A+ = 9, A = 8, B+ = 7, B = 6, C = 5, P = 4, F = 0). SGPA measures semester performance while CGPA measures cumulative degree standing.</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Common across collegiate institutions in India, widely guided by University Grants Commission (UGC) Choice Based Credit System (CBCS) regulations.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4 font-semibold text-[color:var(--fg)]">UK Degree Classifications</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Undergraduate degrees are classified based on cumulative percentage thresholds: First Class (typically 70%+), Upper Second / 2:1 (60–69%), Lower Second / 2:2 (50–59%), and Third Class (40–49%).</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Standard framework throughout England, Wales, Northern Ireland, and Scotland.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4 font-semibold text-[color:var(--fg)]">ECTS Framework</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Credit transfer and accumulation framework measuring student workload (60 ECTS credits per full-time academic year). Does not prescribe a universal mathematical GPA scale.</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">European Higher Education Area (EHEA) member institutions.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4 font-semibold text-[color:var(--fg)]">Percentage / Mark Systems</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Raw percentage score calculated as (Marks Obtained ÷ Total Marks) × 100, evaluated against syllabus pass thresholds and division benchmarks without compulsory GPA conversion.</td>
            <td class="p-3.5 sm:p-4">Encountered worldwide in secondary education, national board examinations, and competitive testing.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Country / Regional Guidance -->
  <div class="space-y-4">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)]">Common Grading Practices by Country and Region</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--muted)]">
      The following overview summarizes patterns frequently observed in major educational jurisdictions. Remember that individual universities and programs maintain their own independent regulations.
    </p>

    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-4 space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)] flex items-center justify-between">
          <span>Bangladesh</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-normal text-[color:var(--accent)]">4.0 Scale</span>
        </h4>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)] leading-relaxed">
          Many universities use a 4.0 GPA/CGPA system, but exact grade boundaries can vary by university or program. Public universities and private institutions often establish specific percentage bands for letter grades (e.g. 80%+ for A+ or 4.00), while passing criteria and honors distinctions differ across faculties.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-4 space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)] flex items-center justify-between">
          <span>United States</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-normal text-[color:var(--accent)]">4.0 Scale</span>
        </h4>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)] leading-relaxed">
          A 4.0 GPA system is very common, but institutions may use different grading and GPA calculation rules. Some colleges award an A+ with 4.0 points while others assign 4.33; some omit plus/minus modifiers entirely; and honors classifications (e.g. Cum Laude) follow institution-specific percentile cutoffs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-4 space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)] flex items-center justify-between">
          <span>India</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-normal text-[color:var(--accent)]">10.0 Scale</span>
        </h4>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)] leading-relaxed">
          10-point GPA/CGPA systems are common, but exact grade-point and percentage-conversion rules vary by institution. While the UGC CBCS guideline suggests a 10-point grading matrix, individual university statutes establish their own conversion formulas (such as percentage = CGPA × 9.5 or other official multipliers).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-4 space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)] flex items-center justify-between">
          <span>Australia</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-normal text-[color:var(--accent)]">7.0 Scale / WAM</span>
        </h4>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)] leading-relaxed">
          7-point GPA systems are used by some institutions, while others may use different measures such as Weighted Average Mark (WAM) or percentage scores out of 100. Always check whether your Australian institution reports GPA on a 7.0 scale, a 4.0 scale, or exclusively relies on WAM for honors eligibility.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-4 space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)] flex items-center justify-between">
          <span>United Kingdom</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-normal text-[color:var(--accent)]">Honours Classes</span>
        </h4>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)] leading-relaxed">
          UK higher education commonly uses degree classifications such as First Class, 2:1, 2:2, and Third Class based on module percentages. Some institutions may also use GPA systems alongside classifications, but traditional honours degrees evaluate overall performance against percentage benchmarks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-4 space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)] flex items-center justify-between">
          <span>Europe & ECTS</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-normal text-[color:var(--accent)]">National + ECTS</span>
        </h4>
        <p class="text-[color:var(--muted)] leading-relaxed">
          There is no single universal European GPA scale. Grading practices vary by country and institution (e.g. 1–5 scale in Germany where 1 is best, 0–20 in France, 1–10 in the Netherlands). ECTS is primarily a credit-transfer and accumulation framework rather than a universal mathematical GPA scale.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Custom Standard Guidance -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--border)] bg-[color:var(--surface)] p-5 sm:p-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-base font-bold text-[color:var(--fg)]">
      <Icon name="gear" class="w-5 h-5 text-[color:var(--accent)]" />
      <h4>When to Use the Custom Standard</h4>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--muted)]">
      If your institution uses a grading table that is not listed, use the Custom Standard and enter the grade boundaries and GPA values from your official institutional policy. The Custom Standard allows you to define your school's exact letter grades, percentage thresholds, and numeric grade points without forcing your academic record into an incompatible model.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
